WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:07.600
So, these tools of inferential
statistics for testing differences

2
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.040
between means, whether the t-test
or its nonparametric equivalents,

3
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.120
and especially ANOVA, ultimately
seem like fairly simple tools,

4
00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:23.620
but they are actually the basis
of all the experimental science

5
00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:27.620
developed in fields such as
agronomy and pharmaceuticals

6
00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:33.920
over a very long time. We design
experiments to try to examine

7
00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:38.660
the effect of one or more factors
on the variable of interest:

8
00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:43.280
plant growth, or a drug's effect
on blood pressure, for example.

9
00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.140
So, these really are the
tools that allow us to test

10
00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:54.900
whether a treatment significantly
affects what we are interested in.

11
00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:00.720
These are also fields where
conducting an experiment is very

12
00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:08.140
expensive, so we would like to
optimize our experiments and how

13
00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:12.320
we conduct them, which brings us to
the concept of experimental design.

14
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:17.600
We will look at an example
in agronomy: suppose you

15
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:22.820
had 16 plots available and
wanted to test whether different

16
00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:27.540
types of fertilizer have a positive
effect on the growth of these plants,

17
00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:31.100
and whether variations in

18
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:34.280
sunlight and moisture between
plots also have an effect.

19
00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:41.520
So, in a first example, we
randomly assign one of the

20
00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.840
fertilizer types to each of the 16
plots. This means we might have,

21
00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:50.740
in the first row, for example,
the same fertilizer twice and no

22
00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:57.680
fertilizer C, or here, fertilizer A
twice, but no fertilizer B, and so on.

23
00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.060
Each type of fertilizer can occur at random.

24
00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:08.440
Fertilizer C also occurs five times, and A

25
00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:17.700
four times, B four times,
and D only three times,

26
00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:21.800
across the experiment as
a whole. So, ultimately,

27
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:27.940
the degree of representation
differs between our fertilizers.

28
00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:34.760
This is an experimental design that
we generate entirely at random.

29
00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.140
In the second example, called a
Latin square design, we arrange for

30
00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:44.580
each fertilizer to occur in every row
and column. This also gives us a much

31
00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:48.940
better representation of the
variations we can observe horizontally

32
00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:53.480
with the moisture level or
sunlight level on our plots.

33
00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:58.680
Finally, we might also
prefer to focus on one of the

34
00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:02.820
covariates of change, namely
moisture, and arrange for

35
00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:07.400
all fertilizers to occur at
every moisture level, but

36
00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:11.800
choose their positions randomly
with respect to sunlight.

37
00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:17.200
We will improve the quality of
our sampling, our experimental

38
00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:22.460
design, to interpret differences
between moisture blocks.

39
00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.640
Here is how this translates
into mathematical terms.

40
00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:32.020
Here, with complete randomization,
we have our experiment,

41
00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:36.200
in which we randomly assigned
the fertilizers to the plots.

42
00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:40.300
We can therefore write this as
a standard fixed-effects model,

43
00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:46.300
where the growth value we expect in one plot

44
00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:49.000
or another depends on the
mean growth across all

45
00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:54.260
the plots, plus the effect associated
with the fertilizer type used.

46
00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:01.160
In the second example, the Latin square,

47
00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:08.640
we can distinguish a fixed effect
associated with the fertilizer

48
00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:13.280
and random effects, where
we chose to distribute

49
00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:18.180
the fertilizers randomly within
each row and each column.

50
00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:23.300
So, we can also test the effects of the rows

51
00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:26.460
and columns, in addition to the
fixed effect of the fertilizer.

52
00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:31.280
Here, we start talking about linear
mixed models, which we can write

53
00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:35.740
in R, using mixed-model
functions, where we have the

54
00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:40.400
fixed effect of fertilizer and
random effects for rows and columns.

55
00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:44.600
Finally, in the last example,
which is somewhat similar,

56
00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:49.240
we have blocks, and we will have the
fixed effect of fertilizer plus the

57
00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:53.280
random effect of the block. We will not
be able to say much about the effect

58
00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:59.020
of columns in our result, but we
will have strengthened the inference,

59
00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:03.120
the quality of the tests we perform
on the block associated with moisture.
